+# Design analysis for the D13 variant
+# a) Concrete behavior:
+#    The edge palette still has two aligned color lines, but the pair must now be read in
+#    different directions depending on where a hollow sprite sits relative to that edge.
+#    Example with a top palette:
+#      top row    : [2, 4, 6]
+#      second row : [7, 8, 3]
+#    A sprite in the upper half with border 2 gets fill 7 (forward lookup 2->7).
+#    A sprite in the lower half with border 8 gets fill 4 (reverse lookup 8->4).
+#    The original task always used 2->7 and would leave the lower-half border-8 sprite
+#    unchanged because 8 was not a key. The variant therefore changes the fill decision
+#    exactly at the "choose mapped fill color" step; palette detection and hole detection
+#    remain the same.
+# b) Complexity calibration:
+#    To keep the reverse rule visible, palette border colors and palette fill colors are
+#    forced to be disjoint. Each generated sample contains at least one forward-using sprite
+#    and one reverse-using sprite, plus one distractor. This avoids degeneration into the
+#    original single-direction behavior and keeps the number of moving parts small.
+# c) Old implicit assumptions that break:
+#    - The old _palette_mapping assumed only outer-line colors would ever key a fill.
+#      That fails once opposite-side sprites use inner-line colors as border colors.
+#    - The old fill loop only tested "adjacent color in mapping". That silently skips valid
+#      reverse cases, so the new solver must choose forward or reverse first, then test keys.
+#    - The old generator allowed overlap between border-key and fill-value sets. That would
+#      make forward and reverse lookups visually ambiguous, so the variant forbids overlap.
+# d) Placement legality:
+#    Sprites and the palette still keep a one-cell moat via the occupancy mask. In addition,
+#    forward sprites are placed wholly in the palette-side half of the grid and reverse
+#    sprites wholly in the far half, judged by their hole centroids. This guarantees that the
+#    solver's midpoint-based side test matches the intended role.
+# e) Visual observability:
+#    Human solvers can now see two supporting cases in each sample: a near-edge sprite whose
+#    border color appears on the palette's outer line, and a far-side sprite whose border
+#    color appears on the inner line. The side split is spatial and visible; it does not rely
+#    on color ids or hidden ordering.
+# f) Visual cleanliness:
+#    The grid keeps only three sprites per sample (forward, reverse, distractor). Slightly
+#    larger minimum grids and side-aware placement leave clear empty space between objects, so
+#    the palette and each sprite remain legible.
+# g) Color-rule inferability:
